Two materials emerged as the most compelling lightweight candidates: boron carbide (B4​C\mathrm{B\_{4}C}) and boron suboxide (B6​O\mathrm{B\_{6}O}). Both lie at the extreme low-density end of the dataset while maintaining very high stiffness. B4​C\mathrm{B\_{4}C} has a density of approximately 2.54​g/cm32.54\,\mathrm{g/cm^{3}} and a bulk modulus of 238​GPa238\,\mathrm{GPa}, while B6​O\mathrm{B\_{6}O} has a density of 2.62​g/cm32.62\,\mathrm{g/cm^{3}} and a bulk modulus of 229​GPa229\,\mathrm{GPa}. These compounds are well-known superhard ceramics and appear as clear outliers in the stiffness-density landscape.

